    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Squaring a semicircle’s square-root equation can introduce points on the other half of the circle, so check candidates in the original equation. For a full circle, direct substitution into its implicit equation includes both halves and does not cause this particular branch problem.",
      "worked": "A semicircle’s square-root equation specifies one half of the circle. Squaring removes its sign restriction, so a candidate intersection may lie on the other half. Substitute each candidate into the original equation and reject any point outside the given semicircle.\n\nFor a full circle, substitute the line directly into the implicit circle equation. Both halves are already included, so this particular loss of a branch restriction does not occur.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
